x86_64-linux  there is nothing to compare here, by construction: the case includes <new>, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all and refuses the file outright. Every name that reaches the runtime - _ZSt15set_new_handlerPFvvE, _ZSt15get_new_handlerv, _ZSt7nothrow, _ZnwmRKSt9nothrow_t, _ZnamRKSt9nothrow_t, _ZTISt9bad_alloc, _ZTISt9exception, _ZNSt9bad_allocD1Ev - is compared on arm64-darwin below and agrees, the Itanium spelling being one spelling on both hosts.
arm64-darwin  a difference about emission and not about names, in three parts. cpp11 emits <new>'s inline constructors and their copies, the placement operators as the inline functions the header defines them as, its own vector-constructor loop (___cxx1_vec_new_4Loud) and a memset for a value-initialised array, where clang inlines or folds each. cpp11 copies a thrown temporary into the exception object where clang builds it in place, so cpp11 calls the runtime's ~bad_alloc and ~bad_array_new_length on the temporary and guards the copy with __cxa_free_exception, and clang has no such calls. And clang names the nothrow deletes, _ZdlPvRKSt9nothrow_t and _ZdaPvRKSt9nothrow_t: [expr.new]/20's cleanup, freeing the storage when a constructor throws inside a `new (std::nothrow)` expression, which cpp11 does not write for any form of new - a recorded gap, not a name. Every name both compilers emit agrees.
x86_64-windows  there is nothing to compare here, by construction: the case includes a C++ standard library header, and on the cross targets clang has no C++ headers at all and refuses the file outright. What this target emits is checked on the Windows box instead.
